## Abstract

This document states the WIN Paradigm in the language of the Standard
Model. It avoids the framework's own terminology — the "substrate," the
"mesh," the "N/4 value," the "shell" — and describes the same physical
content using only Standard Model concepts. The result is a compact
statement of what the framework claims, what it derives, and what it
takes as empirical, expressed in terms a particle physicist would
recognize without translation.

The central claim is that the spacetime dimension d = 4 is the only
input required to derive the Standard Model's gauge structure, matter
content, and generation count. Everything else follows by arithmetic
from that single number.

---

## 1. The Dimension Is the Only Input

The Standard Model of particle physics rests on four-dimensional
spacetime. It assumes d = 4. It does not derive it. Every calculation in
the Standard Model — from the running of the gauge couplings to the
prediction of the Higgs branching ratios — is performed in four
dimensions, and four is treated as an input.

The WIN Paradigm treats the dimension differently. It treats d = 4 as a
**derived** quantity — a consequence of the photon's own structure.

A photon is a massless spin-1 particle. It has exactly two helicity
states: right-circular and left-circular. Each helicity state is
described by a complex amplitude, which has two real components. Two
helicities times two real components gives four real numbers. Those four
real numbers are the photon's phase space.

The Wick rotation of this phase space — a standard procedure in quantum
field theory, guaranteed by the Osterwalder-Schrader theorem — produces
a four-dimensional spacetime with Lorentzian signature. The four
dimensions of the phase space become the four dimensions of spacetime.

**The dimension of the universe is not an assumption. It is the phase
space of light.**

This is the only place in the framework where a physical input enters.
Everything else — the substrate, the gauge structure, the particle
content, the generations, the masses — is derived from d = 4 by
arithmetic.

