## 2. The Gauge Structure

The Standard Model's gauge group is SU(3) × SU(2) × U(1). Its rank — the
number of commuting generators — is four: two from SU(3), one from
SU(2), one from U(1). This is not an accident. It is the first hint that
the Standard Model's structure is tied to the dimensionality of
spacetime.

To include the right-handed neutrino — required by the existence of
neutrino masses — the grand unified group must have rank at least five.
The smallest simple group with rank five and a sixteen-dimensional
spinor representation is SO(10). This is a theorem in Lie group theory.
No other group of rank five has a 16-spinor.

The rank of SO(10) is exactly the number of partitions of 4. The
partition function p(d), which counts the ways of writing d as a sum of
positive integers, satisfies p(4) = 5. The equation p(d) = d + 1 has a
unique solution at d = 4. For every other dimension, the partition
number exceeds d + 1 or falls short.

**The combinatorial structure of the number 4 selects SO(10).** The
gauge group of the Standard Model is a subgroup of SO(10), and SO(10) is
selected by the partition structure of the spacetime dimension.

