## 4. The Substrate

The dimension d = 4 fixes the total number of fermionic degrees of
freedom in the substrate. That number is N = d · 2^d = 4 · 16 = 64.

The substrate has a hidden sector and a visible sector. The hidden
sector is H = 2^d = 16 — one dimension's worth of fermions per degree of
freedom. The visible sector is V = (d−1) · 2^d = 3 · 16 = 48 — three
generations' worth of 16-spinors.

These three numbers — 64, 16, 48 — are determined by d = 4 alone. They
are not parameters. They are arithmetic.

The substrate is realized as an 8×8 lattice with periodic boundary
conditions — a torus. The number 64 equals 8², so the 64 modes of the
substrate are the 64 momentum states of a two-dimensional torus. The
coordination number of the lattice is four, matching the dimension of
spacetime.

**The substrate is not a new object introduced by the framework. It is
the set of 64 fermionic modes that d = 4 requires, arranged on the
simplest two-dimensional lattice that accommodates them.**

