## 5. The Particle Multiplet

The Laplacian operator on the 8×8 torus has 64 eigenmodes with 13
distinct eigenvalues. The spectrum is symmetric under the mirror map
λ → 8 − λ. Every eigenvalue comes in a pair (λ, 8 − λ) of equal
multiplicity, except λ = 4, which is the fixed point of the mirror map
and has multiplicity fourteen.

The mirror symmetry is exact. It is a geometric property of the torus.
No fine-tuning is required.

The 14 modes at λ = 4 are the modes that do not cancel under the mirror
map. They are the **residual** of the spectrum — the modes that survive
when every paired mode annihilates its mirror.

**These 14 modes are the Standard Model particle multiplet.** They
include five bosonic states (the dark photon, the τ, the W, the Z, the
Higgs) and nine fermionic states (the neutrino, the electron, the muon,
the up, down, strange, charm, bottom, and top quarks).

The 14 modes split into three orbits under the symmetry group of the
torus. The orbit sizes are 2, 8, and 4. The 2-mode orbit contains the
neutrino and the electron. The 8-mode orbit contains the middle-mass
fermions. The 4-mode orbit contains the heavy bosons and the top quark.

**The particle content of one Standard Model generation is the residual
of a self-canceling vacuum on the 8×8 torus. The particle count of 14 is
the multiplicity at the self-paired eigenvalue. Nothing is fitted.**

