## 6. The Three Generations

The Standard Model has three generations of fermions. The WIN Paradigm
derives this count as d − 1 = 3.

The 16-spinor of SO(10) describes one generation. To get three
generations, the framework relies on the three **spin structures** of
the torus — the three ways the boundary conditions of the substrate can
be chosen, corresponding to periodic or anti-periodic behavior in each
of the two torus directions, modulo the equivalence that gives a
distinct third class.

Each spin structure carries its own copy of the 16-spinor. The three
copies are the three generations.

The three generations are **not identical**. They differ in the winding
depth of their modes on the torus. Modes with more winding have higher
mass. This is why the three generations have different masses despite
having identical gauge quantum numbers.

**The number of generations is not a parameter. It is d − 1, fixed by
the dimension. The mass hierarchy between generations comes from the
winding structure of the torus, not from a separate mechanism.**

