## 7. The Cosmological Constant

In quantum field theory, the vacuum energy density is computed as a sum
over all field modes, each contributing half its energy. The sum
diverges at the Planck scale, and the result is roughly 10^120 times
larger than the observed value. This is the cosmological constant
problem. The cancellation mechanism that reduces the enormous sum to
the observed value is not known.

The WIN Paradigm provides a geometric mechanism. The 8×8 torus has a
mirror symmetry λ → 8 − λ. Every mode pairs with a mirror partner of
equal multiplicity. The zero-point contributions of paired modes cancel
exactly. The only modes that survive are the 14 at λ = 4, the fixed
point of the mirror map.

The residual of the cancellation is exactly 28 out of 128 — that is,
7/32 of the total. This is a geometric fact, not a fine-tuned
cancellation.

**The vacuum energy is not canceled by an unknown high-energy
mechanism. It is canceled by the mirror symmetry of the substrate.
The observed particle content is what remains after the cancellation.**

