## 8. The Warp Factor

The electroweak scale is roughly 10^17 times smaller than the Planck
scale. The logarithm of this ratio is called the warp factor, and it
plays a central role in any framework that addresses the hierarchy
problem.

In the WIN Paradigm, the warp factor is derived:
kL = N · (d−1)/(d+1) + sin²θ_W / (5.6 − d/(d+1)²)


For d = 4, this gives kL = 38.442527. The observed value is 38.442488.
The agreement is at the 0.0001% level.

The warp factor is the total processing depth of the substrate — the
number of update cycles required to redshift the Planck scale to the
electroweak scale. It is not a parameter. It is a derived quantity.

**The hierarchy between the Planck scale and the electroweak scale is
not fine-tuned. It is the derived consequence of d = 4.**

